{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1343 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "29642fb9fc5c9526ef9e4f0e2a9e59d99ae9171d5f2feba7c9c767afe56e297d" :edb/db-before-t 1342 :edb/db-after-t 1343 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534655 50 #inst "2026-10-12T06:10:40.003Z" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186146564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146565 13194139534655 17592186045417 false] [17592186146564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146566 13194139534655 17592186045417 false] [17592186146564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146567 13194139534655 17592186045417 false] [17592186146564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146568 13194139534655 17592186045417 false] [17592186146564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146569 13194139534655 17592186045417 false] [17592186146564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431123 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186146564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431127 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186146564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431132 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186146564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431137 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186146564 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431139 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186146564 1078 #edb/ref 17592186431137 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186147345 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?\n\n- $y'' + 81y = 0$\n\n- $y'' + 9y'+81y = 0$\n\n- $y'' - 81y = 0$" 13194139534655 17592186045417 false] [17592186147345 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?\n\nI. $y'' + 81y = 0$\n\nII. $y'' + 9y'+81y = 0$\n\nIII. $y'' - 81y = 0$" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186147347 1071 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 9 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y = R\\cos(3t+\\phi_1)$\n\n- $y = R\\sin(9t+\\phi_2)$\n\n- $y = A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3t}$\n\nNote that $R,\\phi_1,\\phi_2, A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139534655 17592186045417 false] [17592186147347 1071 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 9 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y = R\\cos(3t+\\phi_1)$\n\nII. $y = R\\sin(9t+\\phi_2)$\n\nIII. $y = A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3t}$\n\nNote that $R,\\phi_1,\\phi_2, A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186147347 1074 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = 0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 9\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=3$ into the three different forms of the general solution gives the following:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(3t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(3t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y =A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3 t}$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$" 13194139534655 17592186045417 false] [17592186147347 1074 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = 0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 9\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=3$ into the three different forms of the general solution gives the following:\n\n1\\. $y = R\\cos{(3t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y = R\\sin{(3t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y =A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3 t}$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186147349 1071 "A particle of mass $36\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is $y'' + \\dfrac{1}{2}y = 0$\n\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi}\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}$\n\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)},$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase." 13194139534655 17592186045417 false] [17592186147349 1071 "A particle of mass $36\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The governing differential equation is $y'' + \\dfrac{1}{2}y = 0$\n\nII. The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi}\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}$\n\nIII. The general solution can be expressed as $y = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)},$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase." 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186147430 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431144 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186147431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431145 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186147432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431146 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186147703 1050 780.0 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186148135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186148135 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+64y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186148135 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 64y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" 13194139534655 17592186045417 false] [17592186148135 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=64\\,⟹\\,\\omega =8.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\omega }\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{8}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186148753 1064 "**Simple harmonic motion (SHM)** is a special type of periodic motion in which an object oscillates about an equilibrium position. In SHM, the object's acceleration is proportional to the distance from the equilibrium position, and it acts in the opposite direction to the displacement.\n\nIf $y(t)$ represents the position of an object experiencing SHM, then it obeys the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nAssuming $\\omega > 0,$ the general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nFrom the above solution, we see that the **angular frequency** $\\Omega$ equals\n\n$$\n\\Omega = \\omega,\n$$\n\n and the **period** $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\Omega}= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ represents the number of seconds it takes for the object to complete one full oscillation. Similarly, the **frequency** $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) represents the number of times the object oscillates per second and is given by\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{1}{T} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi}.\n$$\n\nIn forms 1 and 2, the constant $R$ is called the **amplitude**, and it represents the object's maximum distance from the equilibrium position.\n\n**Note:** To see where the SHM equation comes from, remember that the object's acceleration $y''$ is proportional to the distance $y$ from the equilibrium and acts in the opposite direction as the displacement. If the constant of proportionality is $\\omega^2,$ then we have\n\n$$\ny'' = - \\omega^2 y \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$" 13194139534655 17592186045417 false] [17592186148753 1064 "**Simple harmonic motion (SHM)** is a special type of periodic motion in which an object oscillates about an equilibrium position. In SHM, the object's acceleration is proportional to the distance from the equilibrium position, and it acts in the opposite direction to the displacement.\n\nIf $y(t)$ represents the position of an object experiencing SHM, then it obeys the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nAssuming $\\omega > 0,$ the general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nFrom the above solution, we see that the **angular frequency** $\\Omega$ equals\n\n$$\n\\Omega = \\omega,\n$$\n\n and the **period** $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\Omega}= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ represents the number of seconds it takes for the object to complete one full oscillation. Similarly, the **frequency** $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) represents the number of times the object oscillates per second and is given by\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{1}{T} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi}.\n$$\n\nIn forms 1 and 2, the constant $R$ is called the **amplitude**, and it represents the object's maximum distance from the equilibrium position.\n\n**Note:** To see where the SHM equation comes from, remember that the object's acceleration $y''$ is proportional to the distance $y$ from the equilibrium and acts in the opposite direction as the displacement. If the constant of proportionality is $\\omega^2,$ then we have\n\n$$\ny'' = - \\omega^2 y \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186357712 1071 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139534655 17592186045417 false] [17592186357712 1071 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186357712 1074 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nTherefore, only function III could be used to represent $y(t).$" 13194139534655 17592186045417 false] [17592186357712 1074 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nTherefore, only function III could be used to represent $y(t).$" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431121 1080 "I only" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431121 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431121 1083 #uuid "0572e053-bbb1-51d9-80e9-c92d6011545b" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431122 1080 "I and II only" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431122 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431122 1083 #uuid "08d858c9-ca22-560b-97eb-40da3122196f" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431123 1080 "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431123 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431123 1083 #uuid "114d6b8f-6471-5003-8bc6-bf96f5a4a260" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431124 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431124 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431124 1083 #uuid "2069605a-a81e-57fb-a295-96c83fa94991" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431125 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431125 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534655 17592186045417 true] [17592186431125 1083 #uuid "2c726ff3-14e5-5477-aa74-d916199a65c4" 13194139534655 17592186